# size 20x4
# A control string this emulator does not act on, followed by text.
#
# vim sends "\eP$qm\e\\" on startup to ask what the current attributes are. The body of one of
# these is ordinary printable text, so a parser that gives up at the ESC P draws it: this put
# "$qm" in the top left corner of a real screen. The terminal has to read it to its end and throw
# it away, which is what "after" arriving in column 1 proves.
\eP$qm\e\\after
\r\n\e_G payload here \e\\apc
\r\n\eX sos body \x07seven
